// `com.microsoft.MatMulNBits` — block-dequantising GEMV.
//
// One workgroup computes `QB_COLS` adjacent output elements `Y[m][n0 .. n0+QB_COLS)`. The grid is
// `(ceil(N / QB_COLS), M, 1)`, so this kernel is *correct* for every `M` and merely unoptimal for
// `M > 1`; prefill is a tiling problem, not a correctness problem, and shipping the correct shape
// first is `OP_COVERAGE.md` §11's rule.
//
// # Why a workgroup produces more than one output
//
// The first shipped version computed exactly one `Y[m][n]` per workgroup. That is the clearest
// possible expression of the reduction, and on the measured trace it left the discrete part at
// ~18% of its memory bandwidth and ~12% of its ALU throughput *simultaneously* — the signature of
// a latency-bound kernel, not a bandwidth-bound one. Every cause was **per output element**
// rather than per multiply-add:
//
//   * **The activation row was re-read once per output column.** Every workgroup streams the
//     whole `A[m][0..K)`. With one column per workgroup that is `N` reads of the row *as load
//     instructions*; the bytes hit L1 but the instructions still issue. `QB_COLS` columns per
//     workgroup amortises those loads by `QB_COLS` exactly, because the same `A` values feed
//     every column of the tile — the innermost loop is now over columns, inside the `A` load.
//   * **The reduction tree and its barriers were paid once per output element.** `log2(wg)`
//     barriers for ~32 multiply-adds per thread is a ratio no amount of occupancy hides. The tile
//     reduces `QB_COLS` columns inside the *same* barrier sequence, so barriers per output fall
//     by `QB_COLS` with no extra synchronisation and no extra barrier.
//   * **The scale multiplied every element.** `acc += a * (q - zp) * s` is one multiply per
//     weight; the block partial sum is now accumulated first and scaled once per (column, block),
//     which is `QB_BLOCK`× fewer multiplies. See the accuracy note below — this changes the
//     rounding and the falsifier for that is `model_output_equivalence`, not an argument.
//   * **fp16 activations were unpacked one lane at a time.** `unpackHalf2x16` yields both lanes;
//     the 4-bit path consumes `k` in adjacent pairs, so the second lane was being fetched by a
//     second load of the same word. `load_a2` returns the pair.
//   * **Every output store was a two-atomic read-modify-write.** Unavoidable when neighbouring
//     `Y` lanes belong to different workgroups. With an even `QB_COLS` the tile owns both lanes of
//     every word it writes, so the store is a plain aligned `uint` write. A discrete part barely
//     notices; on an integrated part global atomics are expensive and this was `N` of them per
//     dispatch.
//
// The workgroup size is also now chosen to *divide* `blocks_per_col` rather than merely to cover
// it, so no invocation sits out the reduction it still has to synchronise with — at `K = 3072`
// the old rule picked 128 invocations for 96 blocks and idled a quarter of them. See `quant.rs`'s
// `gemv_workgroup`.
//
// # The three things this file deliberately does not do
//
// 1. **No subgroup operations.** Both development GPUs report a subgroup size of 32, which is the
//    strongest possible invitation to bake 32 in and pass every local test — and lavapipe reports
//    **8**. `VkPhysicalDeviceSubgroupProperties::subgroupSize` is not guaranteed to be anything,
//    so the cross-workgroup reduction is a shared-memory tree sized by `gl_WorkGroupSize.x`.
// 2. **No 16-bit storage capability.** fp16 activations are read and written through
//    `unpackHalf2x16`/`packHalf2x16` over `uint` buffers, which is core GLSL 4.2 and needs no
//    device feature at all. `shaderFloat16` and `storageBuffer16BitAccess` therefore gate nothing
//    here. This matters more than it looks: the Phi-3.5 census found all 161 `MatMulNBits` nodes
//    carry **fp16** activations, scales and outputs, so an f32-only kernel would decline the model
//    this kernel exists to run. Accumulation is fp32 regardless of storage, which is also what
//    ORT's `SQNBIT_CompFp32` path does — see `quant.rs`'s `accuracy_level` note.
// 3. **No dequantised weight in memory.** `B` is unpacked into registers inside the block loop and
//    never written anywhere. This is the P-series constraint from §8.2 and it is the reason int4
//    is worth having at all.
//
// # Accuracy
//
// Hoisting `s` out of the element loop changes the summation order: the block partial sum is
// formed in fp32 over `QB_BLOCK` unscaled terms and scaled once, rather than each term being
// scaled before it is added. Both are fp32 accumulations of the same exact products, and the
// hoisted form has strictly *fewer* rounding steps and sums terms that share a magnitude class
// because they share a scale. That is an argument, not evidence. The evidence is the falsifier
// that already exists — `model_output_equivalence` against the CPU EP on the real model — and it
// must stay `MATCH`.
//
// # Layout, derived from the ORT CPU EP rather than from the schema prose
//
// Established empirically (feed `A = I` so each output row is a dequantised weight column) —
// `OP_COVERAGE.md` §8.1.1:
//
//   * `B` is `[N][blocks_per_col][blob_bytes]`, i.e. **row `n` is output column `n`**, so
//     `Y[m][n] = sum_k A[m][k] * dequant(B[n][k])`. `B` is transposed relative to `MatMul`.
//   * 4-bit nibble order is **low nibble first**: element `2i` is `byte[i] & 0xF`, element `2i+1`
//     is `byte[i] >> 4`.
//   * When `zero_points` is absent the implied zero point is `1 << (bits-1)` — 8 at 4 bits.
//   * `zero_points` is packed the same way as `B` at 4 bits (two blocks per byte, low nibble
//     first), one byte per block at 8 bits, with each column's run padded to a whole byte.
//   * `scales` is indexed `n * blocks_per_col + blk`.
//
// # Specialisation constants
//
//   id 0  local_size_x      power of two in [32, 256]; the host picks the largest that divides
//                           `blocks_per_col`, capped at the 256-invocation floor of §7.2.
//   id 1  QB_BITS           4 or 8. A spec constant rather than a `-D` define on purpose: both
//                           bit widths are then one SPIR-V source and one pipeline-cache key, so
//                           gpt-oss's mixed 4/8-bit graph is not a second shader.
//   id 2  QB_BLOCK          16/32/64/128.
//   id 3  QB_HAS_ZP         0 when the node has no `zero_points` input.
//   id 4  QB_COLS           output columns per workgroup, 1..QB_MAX_COLS. The host guarantees
//                           `local_size_x * QB_COLS <= QB_RED_WORDS`, which is what keeps the
//                           shared-memory requirement a static property of this module rather
//                           than a device-dependent one.
//
// # Bindings
//
//   0  A            activations, `[.., M, K]`
//   1  B            packed weights, addressed as `uint` words
//   2  scales       `[N * blocks_per_col]`
//   3  zero_points  packed bytes. **Bound even when absent** — the translate handler binds
//                   `scales` a second time as an inert placeholder, because a declared descriptor
//                   must be bound whether or not `QB_HAS_ZP` folds every read away. Nothing reads
//                   it when `QB_HAS_ZP == 0`.
//   4  Y            `[.., M, N]`

#version 450
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require

layout(local_size_x_id = 0, local_size_x = 256) in;

layout(constant_id = 1) const uint QB_BITS = 4u;
layout(constant_id = 2) const uint QB_BLOCK = 32u;
layout(constant_id = 3) const uint QB_HAS_ZP = 0u;
layout(constant_id = 4) const uint QB_COLS = 1u;

// Largest column tile this module can hold in registers. Every per-column array below is sized by
// this literal, never by the specialisation constant, so the register budget is a static property
// of the module. `quant.rs` clamps `QB_COLS` to it.
#define QB_MAX_COLS 8u

layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
    uint m_total;         // product of all leading dims of A times M
    uint k;               // reduction extent
    uint n;               // output columns
    uint blocks_per_col;  // k / QB_BLOCK
} pc;

// -- bindings ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#ifdef DTYPE_F16
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) readonly buffer InA { uint data[]; } ina;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) readonly buffer InS { uint data[]; } ins;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4) buffer OutY { uint data[]; } outy;

float load_a(uint i) { return unpackHalf2x16(ina.data[i >> 1u])[i & 1u]; }

// Both fp16 lanes of the word holding elements `i` and `i+1`. `i` must be even — every caller
// indexes `k` in adjacent pairs starting at a multiple of `QB_BLOCK`, and `a_base = row * k` with
// `k` a multiple of `block_size`, so evenness holds by construction rather than by luck.
vec2 load_a2(uint i) { return unpackHalf2x16(ina.data[i >> 1u]); }

float load_s(uint i) { return unpackHalf2x16(ins.data[i >> 1u])[i & 1u]; }

// Store one fp16 lane without disturbing its neighbour. The two lanes of a word may be written by
// two different workgroups, so this is a read-modify-write on device memory — but on **disjoint
// bit lanes**, which is race-free in any interleaving. Same argument as the byte-typed stores in
// `indexing.glsl`, and the same requirement on the allocator: the buffer must be rounded up to a
// whole `uint`. The claim predicate additionally requires `m_total * n` to be even, so the final
// word is never partial for any node we claim.
void store_y(uint i, float v) {
    uint lane = (i & 1u) * 16u;
    uint half_bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, 0.0)) & 0xFFFFu;
    atomicAnd(outy.data[i >> 1u], ~(0xFFFFu << lane));
    atomicOr(outy.data[i >> 1u], half_bits << lane);
}

// Write both lanes of the word holding elements `i` and `i+1` in one non-atomic store. Legal only
// when this workgroup owns *both* elements, which the caller establishes from `QB_COLS` and the
// tile's base offset — never assumed.
void store_y_pair(uint i, float v0, float v1) {
    outy.data[i >> 1u] = packHalf2x16(vec2(v0, v1));
}
#else
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) readonly buffer InA { float data[]; } ina;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2) readonly buffer InS { float data[]; } ins;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4) writeonly buffer OutY { float data[]; } outy;

float load_a(uint i) { return ina.data[i]; }
vec2 load_a2(uint i) { return vec2(ina.data[i], ina.data[i + 1u]); }
float load_s(uint i) { return ins.data[i]; }
void store_y(uint i, float v) { outy.data[i] = v; }
void store_y_pair(uint i, float v0, float v1) {
    outy.data[i] = v0;
    outy.data[i + 1u] = v1;
}
#endif

layout(set = 0, binding = 1) readonly buffer InB { uint data[]; } inb;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3) readonly buffer InZ { uint data[]; } inz;

// -- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Bytes of packed weight per (column, block).
uint blob_bytes() { return (QB_BLOCK * QB_BITS) / 8u; }

/// Bytes of packed zero point per column, padded to a whole byte as ORT pads it.
uint zp_bytes_per_col() { return ((pc.blocks_per_col * QB_BITS) + 7u) / 8u; }

/// The zero point for column `col`, block `blk`.
int load_zp(uint col, uint blk) {
    if (QB_HAS_ZP == 0u) {
        return int(1u << (QB_BITS - 1u));
    }
    uint byte_index;
    uint shift;
    if (QB_BITS == 4u) {
        byte_index = col * zp_bytes_per_col() + (blk >> 1u);
        shift = (blk & 1u) * 4u;
    } else {
        byte_index = col * zp_bytes_per_col() + blk;
        shift = 0u;
    }
    uint word = inz.data[byte_index >> 2u];
    uint b = (word >> ((byte_index & 3u) * 8u)) & 0xFFu;
    return int((b >> shift) & ((1u << QB_BITS) - 1u));
}

// -- reduction --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Shared floats reserved for the reduction: 1024 = 4 KiB, comfortably inside the 16 KiB
// shared-memory floor of §7.2 and inside the 32 KiB an Intel Xe subslice offers without
// collapsing occupancy. Sized by the literal rather than by `local_size_x * QB_COLS` so the
// storage requirement is a static property of the module and cannot become device-dependent.
// `quant.rs` guarantees `local_size_x * QB_COLS <= 1024`.
#define QB_RED_WORDS 1024
shared float red[QB_RED_WORDS];

void main() {
    uint col0 = gl_WorkGroupID.x * QB_COLS;  // first output column of this tile
    uint row = gl_WorkGroupID.y;             // m
    uint tid = gl_LocalInvocationID.x;
    uint wg = gl_WorkGroupSize.x;
    uint bpc = pc.blocks_per_col;

    // Per-column state, hoisted out of the block loop. A column past the end of `N` — only
    // possible in the tail tile when `QB_COLS` does not divide `N` — is redirected to `col0`
    // rather than clamped away, so the inner loop stays branch-free and uniform; its result is
    // discarded at the store, which is the only place `N` is re-checked.
    float acc[QB_MAX_COLS];
    uint ucol[QB_MAX_COLS];
    for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
        acc[c] = 0.0;
        ucol[c] = ((col0 + c) < pc.n) ? (col0 + c) : col0;
    }

    if (row < pc.m_total && col0 < pc.n) {
        uint a_base = row * pc.k;
        uint bb = blob_bytes();
        uint nwords = bb >> 2u;

        for (uint blk = tid; blk < bpc; blk += wg) {
            uint k0 = blk * QB_BLOCK;

            // Scale and zero point are invariant across the block. Loading them here rather than
            // per element is what makes the inner loop pure multiply-add.
            float s[QB_MAX_COLS];
            float zpf[QB_MAX_COLS];
            float bacc[QB_MAX_COLS];
            for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
                s[c] = load_s(ucol[c] * bpc + blk);
                zpf[c] = float(load_zp(ucol[c], blk));
                bacc[c] = 0.0;
            }

            for (uint w = 0u; w < nwords; ++w) {
                // `A` is loaded ONCE per word of `B` and reused by every column of the tile. This
                // is the whole point of the tile: the column loop is inside the `A` load, not
                // outside it.
                if (QB_BITS == 4u) {
                    uint ke = a_base + k0 + w * 8u;
                    vec2 a01 = load_a2(ke);
                    vec2 a23 = load_a2(ke + 2u);
                    vec2 a45 = load_a2(ke + 4u);
                    vec2 a67 = load_a2(ke + 6u);
                    for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
                        uint packed = inb.data[(((ucol[c] * bpc + blk) * bb) >> 2u) + w];
                        float z = zpf[c];
                        uint b0 = packed & 0xFFu;
                        uint b1 = (packed >> 8u) & 0xFFu;
                        uint b2 = (packed >> 16u) & 0xFFu;
                        uint b3 = packed >> 24u;
                        float t = a01.x * (float(b0 & 0xFu) - z);
                        t += a01.y * (float(b0 >> 4u) - z);
                        t += a23.x * (float(b1 & 0xFu) - z);
                        t += a23.y * (float(b1 >> 4u) - z);
                        t += a45.x * (float(b2 & 0xFu) - z);
                        t += a45.y * (float(b2 >> 4u) - z);
                        t += a67.x * (float(b3 & 0xFu) - z);
                        t += a67.y * (float(b3 >> 4u) - z);
                        bacc[c] += t;
                    }
                } else {
                    uint ke = a_base + k0 + w * 4u;
                    vec2 a01 = load_a2(ke);
                    vec2 a23 = load_a2(ke + 2u);
                    for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
                        uint packed = inb.data[(((ucol[c] * bpc + blk) * bb) >> 2u) + w];
                        float z = zpf[c];
                        float t = a01.x * (float(packed & 0xFFu) - z);
                        t += a01.y * (float((packed >> 8u) & 0xFFu) - z);
                        t += a23.x * (float((packed >> 16u) & 0xFFu) - z);
                        t += a23.y * (float(packed >> 24u) - z);
                        bacc[c] += t;
                    }
                }
            }

            for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
                acc[c] += bacc[c] * s[c];
            }
        }
    }

    // Shared-memory tree, one pass for the whole tile. `wg` is a power of two (the host guarantees
    // it) and every barrier below is reached by every invocation in the workgroup — the early-out
    // above leaves `acc` at zero rather than returning, precisely so that stays true. Column `c`
    // occupies `red[c * wg .. c * wg + wg)`, so for a fixed `c` consecutive invocations touch
    // consecutive words and the tree stays bank-conflict free exactly as it was with one column.
    for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
        red[c * wg + tid] = acc[c];
    }
    barrier();
    for (uint stride = wg >> 1u; stride > 0u; stride >>= 1u) {
        if (tid < stride) {
            for (uint c = 0u; c < QB_COLS; ++c) {
                red[c * wg + tid] += red[c * wg + tid + stride];
            }
        }
        barrier();
    }

    if (row < pc.m_total && col0 < pc.n) {
        uint base = row * pc.n + col0;
        // The tile owns both lanes of every word it writes when it is an even number of columns,
        // starts on an even element, and no column of it fell off the end of `N`. Then and only
        // then is the read-modify-write unnecessary. This is checked, not assumed, and every term
        // is workgroup-uniform, so the branch is uniform.
        bool paired = (QB_COLS >= 2u) && ((QB_COLS & 1u) == 0u)
                      && ((base & 1u) == 0u) && ((col0 + QB_COLS) <= pc.n);
        if (paired) {
            if (tid < (QB_COLS >> 1u)) {
                store_y_pair(base + tid * 2u, red[(tid * 2u) * wg], red[(tid * 2u + 1u) * wg]);
            }
        } else if (tid < QB_COLS && (col0 + tid) < pc.n) {
            store_y(base + tid, red[tid * wg]);
        }
    }
}
